We can explain our reasoning briefly:

• The four fields have different threat levels and “full‐protection” requirements:
  – Field_3 is highest (0.20) and needs 2 drones – already covered.
  – Field_1 is next (0.12) but has none – it requires 4 drones.
  – Field_2 (0.10) already has 3 protecting plus 1 in transit, but if we reassign we can “afford” to leave Field_2 partially covered.
  – Field_4 (0.06) is lowest and can be left unassigned.
• With only 8 drones available we cannot cover every field fully. To minimize loss we opt to secure the two highest‐priority fields (Field_1 and Field_3) by assigning 4 and 2 drones, respectively.
• That leaves 2 drones which are best assigned to Field_2 (threat 0.10) rather than Field_4 (threat 0.06) so that the residual risk (from the unprotected fields) is minimized.
• Thus our final grouping is as shown above.
